# /split run evidence — byte-immutable.
#
# These ndjson files are verbatim copies of a run's telemetry stream, kept so a
# reported md5 stays re-verifiable from a fresh checkout. `core.autocrlf=true`
# (the default on this Windows host) otherwise rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout:
# split-8f83d7.events.ndjson measured 2752B/daed838f in the index but
# 2767B/236e3761 in a fresh clone, so the published hash no longer matched.
#
# `-text` (not `eol=lf`) because evidence must round-trip unmodified in BOTH
# directions. `eol=lf` guarantees worktree==blob only by normalizing on check-in,
# which silently strips CRs from whatever the run actually produced; measured on
# a CR-bearing fixture, `-text` kept 4f614ce2 end to end while `eol=lf` committed
# 56af49a2. Textual diffs still render — these files contain no NUL bytes, and
# git's diff binary-detection is independent of the `text` attribute.
reports/SPLIT/**/*.ndjson -text

# UserPromptSubmit guard fixture — byte-immutable, same reasoning as above.
# This is a verbatim 5,047B capture of the host `<task-notification>` body that
# leaked into UserPromptSubmit at 2026-09-04T19:02:21Z. Two suites assert on its
# exact byte length, and the mission-compiler NEGATIVE CONTROL pins the goal
# string the compiler extracts from it. `core.autocrlf=true` (this Windows
# host's default) would rewrite its 59 LFs to CRLF on checkout, making a fresh
# clone see 5,106B and shifting every extraction span in the fixture.
plugins/artibot/tests/fixtures/ups-task-notification-2026-09-04.txt -text
